    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8e60b9bb_22f34f82",
        "filename": "fs/incfs/data_mgmt.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 38,
      "author": {
        "id": 1044551
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-06T22:06:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is this wise? I feel that it\u0027s not inconceivable someone might use a \u003e256 page log buffer, at least in some test environment. And this is not space critical. I\u0027m guessing this is to maintain partial compatibility with userspace?",
      "revId": "3ee52ff3367d84807454310cce5ed31732a33a3a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3d788230_e67e53f7",
        "filename": "fs/incfs/data_mgmt.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 38,
      "author": {
        "id": 1080076
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-06T22:17:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "READ_ONCE() can only read up to 8 bytes, making it bigger would force us to write a dedicated getter-setter per field. 256+ buffer in kernel space sounds like utopia to me though, I doubt it can allocate that much ever\nProbably will add a size check in the remount handler\non the other hand, it could be better to have fewer passes before overflowing - 64k passes even over a 4 pages default buffer would give us 585 * 65k \u003d 38 million reads before overflowing. 150 GB should be enough to everyone?",
      "parentUuid": "8e60b9bb_22f34f82",
      "revId": "3ee52ff3367d84807454310cce5ed31732a33a3a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
